<section class="block-section story">
  <span class="section-tag">Story</span>
  <h2>What this page is about</h2>
  <p class="sub">Take-home 1. The unit's anchor question: <em>How do you build for a planet that occasionally tries to destroy what you've built?</em></p>
  <figure class="content-figure">
    <img src="/assets/img/u3/u3-t1-cover.jpg" alt="">
    <figcaption>Image generated with Gemini, AISC. Decorative, not a diagram.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>Earth's surface tells a story 4.5 billion years in the making, and most of it is written in the language of moving plates. Before you can explain an earthquake, a tsunami or an eruption, you have to know what's actually moving and why. This page sets the foundation: Earth's interior, what drives plate motion, and the four settings that produce every major hazard family you'll meet in this unit. You'll also meet your Unit 3 AI partner: <strong>The Plate Tectonics Tutor</strong>, a coach for boundary mechanics that asks questions before it hands you answers.</p>
  <p>By the end you should be able to look at a world map and explain why earthquakes cluster where they do, and why some coasts get tsunamis while others don't.</p>
</section>

